\section*{Supplementary Note S2: Out-of-Sample Tests}

The screen is fitted entirely on Big Island farm cells, so Kaua\okina i provides an independent test. The same 2020 Statewide Agricultural Land Use Baseline \citep{PerroyCollier2021} records ten commercial coffee polygons on Kaua\okina i totalling $\approx$3{,}806 acres---a single estate larger than Ka\okina u's entire farmed acreage---on an island the fitting procedure never sees. We mosaicked the same USGS 3DEP 1/3 arc-second product for Kaua\okina i, reprojected and resampled it to the same 500\,m UTM grid, computed the same eight topographic features by the same code path, and applied the Big Island centroids and 95th-percentile distance threshold unchanged.

The screen classifies 224 of 5{,}986 Kaua\okina i land cells (3.7\%) as Kona-like or Ka\okina u-like, and \textit{none} of them coincides with the 61 cells whose centres fall inside a mapped coffee polygon. Recall is zero, precision is zero, and the enrichment over Kaua\okina i's 1.0\% base rate of coffee land is zero. Repeating the test using only the six features that are not defined relative to the containing island---dropping elevation deviation from scene mean and distance to coastline, both of which are bounded by island size---does not change the outcome.

The reason is substantive rather than technical. Kaua\okina i's coffee sits at a mean elevation of 84\,m on 7\textdegree\ slopes; Kona and Ka\okina u farm cells average 494\,m and 524\,m on 19\textdegree\ and 17\textdegree\ slopes. It is a low-elevation coastal plantation, not a mid-elevation volcanic slope system, and the screen rejects it because it genuinely does not resemble the terrain the screen was built from.

Kaua\okina i tests transfer between islands, which is the harder question but not the one the paper's claims depend on. Those depend on whether the screen generalises to Kona-like ground it was not fitted on, so we test that directly by spatial cross-validation: contiguous latitude bands of the Kona belt are held out in turn, the standardisation, centroids and 95th-percentile threshold are refitted on the remainder, and recovery of the held-out farm cells is measured. Mean recovery is 86\% against an in-sample reference of 94.9\%, so the screen does extend to unseen Kona-like terrain at a cost of roughly nine percentage points. Recovery is uneven, however: across five folds it runs 100, 38, 94, 100 and 100\%, so one segment of the belt is poorly reconstructed from the rest. The screen generalises along the belt on average but not uniformly, and nominations of specific upper-fringe cells (the main text's Results) should be read with that heterogeneity in mind rather than as uniform-confidence predictions.

The screen therefore identifies Kona- and Ka\okina u-like terrain, not coffee-growing terrain, and the largest coffee estate in the United States falls outside it. That is the expected behaviour of an identity-anchored screen rather than an arability mask, and it is evidence that ``coffee country'' is not one terrain signature. It is not confirmation that the screen finds \textit{viable} ground, and the upper-fringe cells nominated in the main text's Results rest on no out-of-sample support.

